{
  "dom_id": "step-q16548",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "For the ellipse \n$\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes.",
  "worked_solution": "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-4$ and $k=3,$ so the center is located at $(-4,3).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=5$ and ${b}^{2}=2.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=5,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=2,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=\\sqrt{5},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{5}=2\\sqrt{5},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=2\\sqrt{2}.$",
